Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{"action": "final", "output": {
  "research_concepts": [
    "cut-heavy shapers",
    "drive-heavy shapers",
    "role portability / transferability of playing style across teams",
    "off-ball (cut) vs on-ball (drive) initiation profiles",
    "structural influence and organizational function reproduction after a move"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to define cut-heavy and drive-heavy shapers from play-type initiation data?",
    "How to measure whether a shaper's role 'travels' better across different teams (outcome metric: e.g., Delta, displacement, structural influence stability)?",
    "How to control for player ability, age, injury, and team system changes that may confound the relationship between cut/drive reliance and portability?",
    "How to ensure sufficient pre- and post-move sample size for shapers, given the small number of movers in this category (n=24)?",
    "How to address potential selection bias where cut-heavy shapers might be less ball-dominant and thus less dependent on specific offensive contexts, making them appear more portable?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-057",
      "status_as_recorded": "supported",
      "finding": "Pre-move cut share positively predicts whether a connector's organizational function reproduces after a move (rho=+0.265 p=0.011 AUC 0.653). Drive share negatively predicts portability (rho=-0.278 p=0.007). Cut-drive difference: rho=+0.314 p=0.002. Top cut tercile 65% portable median Delta +0.167; bottom 35% median -0.039. LOO AUC 0.622; permutation p=0.005. Result robust to dropping individual players. The study used 92 connector movers."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPM-039",
      "status_as_recorded": "supported",
      "finding": "Forecast portability (travel) and fit quadrants predict mover outcomes (embedded minutes, net rating). LOO corr 0.1998, AUC 0.5986; additive embedding hit rate 89.59% vs irrelevant 48.11%; P(add emb > irr)=0.984; P(add net > irr)=0.99; corr(travel,net)=0.1881. Demonstrates that portability can be predicted, but does not address cut vs drive specifically."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-PAP-016",
      "status_as_recorded": "supported",
      "finding": "Portability feasibility gate passed: 296 movers, decoy fraction 1.00, vacuity rho=0.048 (final rho=0.007). Confirms that a decoy-based portability experiment is feasible within the dataset."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-009",
      "what_failed": "Attempt to compare player routing V4 vs V5 direct scoring",
      "why": "Question not answerable with the data/design (untestable). Scoreable 0 of 8 divergences; V5 mostly routed to 'team' rather than a player. Small sample, drift/nonstationarity risk."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-058",
      "what_failed": "Test of season-to-season stability of top sequential suppressors for 30 stars",
      "why": "Rejected (adequately powered null): only 2/30 stars had stable top suppressor across seasons. Sample size limitation and low power for detecting stability."
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "Cut-heavy connectors (players whose presence links teammates' play types) exhibit more portable organizational functions after a team move than drive-heavy connectors.",
    "The effect size is moderate (Spearman rho ~0.27-0.31) and statistically significant in a sample of 92 connector movers.",
    "A decoy-based portability framework is feasible and has been validated for the league-wide dataset."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether the same cut vs drive portability advantage holds specifically for players classified as shapers (low usage, positive residual structural influence) rather than connectors.",
    "Whether the effect is causal or merely reflects that cut-heavy players tend to have more portable, system-independent skills.",
    "Whether the effect size would be large enough to detect in the available sub-sample of n=24 shaper movers.",
    "Whether alternative definitions of 'cut-heavy' (e.g., absolute cut share versus ratio of cut to drive) would yield similar results.",
    "How much of the effect is driven by the defensive attention that drive-heavy players attract, versus inherent skill portability."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The connector category overlaps substantially with shapers (both are low-usage, high-passer/connector types), so the CHT-057 result is likely to generalize to shapers, but the exact definitional difference creates uncertainty.",
    "With an available sample of only 24 shapers who moved teams during the study period, a statistical test of the hypothesis is likely underpowered to detect the moderate effect sizes seen in the connector study, unless a very large effect exists.",
    "The question can be framed as a direct replication of SF-CHT-057 on a sub-population (shapers), which would require little new methodological development but is severely limited by sample size.",
    "A pre-post decoy displacement or structural influence change (Delta) would be the natural outcome metric, consistent with the existing portability framework."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The exact definition of 'connector' used in SF-CHT-057 may differ from the shaper definition provided here; the two sets likely overlap but are not identical.",
    "The n=24 value given as 'units available' could refer to the number of shaper movers who meet minimum possession thresholds; the precise selection criteria (minimum games, possessions per play type) could further reduce the effective sample.",
    "A player's classification as cut-heavy vs drive-heavy could be unstable if based on limited pre-move possessions; measurement reliability may be low for shapers with small play-type volumes.",
    "Potential confounders like age, injury, role change, and team system differences may not be fully controllable in a small sample, increasing the risk of spurious findings."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "yes",
    "explanation": "The discovery of SF-CHT-057, which directly tested the cut vs drive portability hypothesis in a closely related population (connectors), changes the decision from 'proceed with a new test' to 'do_not_proceed' for the exact shaper-specific version. The existing study already provides supporting evidence for the directional claim, and conducting a low-powered replication on a smaller shaper subset (n=24) would likely yield an inconclusive or biased result, adding little value."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.25,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "Only n=24 shaper movers are available; the required sample for 80% power to detect the correlation of ~0.27 observed in connectors is about 100. A large effect (rho > 0.55) would be needed to achieve even 80% power with n=24."
      },
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Cut and drive shares are derived from play-type classification; reliability may be lower for low-usage shapers with fewer initiation possessions, adding noise and further reducing statistical power."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Factors such as player age, injuries, contract situations, and coaching changes may differentially affect drive-heavy vs cut-heavy shapers and are difficult to control in a small observational sample."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "partly",
    "cited": ["FAM:SF-CHT-057"],
    "summary": "The study SF-CHT-057 already tested and supported the hypothesis that cut-heavy players travel better than drive-heavy ones among 'connectors', a structural role likely to overlap heavily with shapers. While the exact shaper definition (low usage, positive residual structural influence) may differ slightly, the available evidence strongly suggests the directional claim holds. A dedicated shaper-level test would be an underpowered replication."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "The stated unit count (n=24) is far below the sample needed to reliably detect an effect of the size seen in connectors (rho ~0.27); any observed effect would need to be implausibly large or would risk being a false positive/negative."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Classifying players as cut-heavy vs drive-heavy from limited pre-move possessions may misclassify borderline players, weakening the signal."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Players who are drive-heavy may also be more reliant on specific teammates or systems; failing to account for fit quality and team strength could attribute differences to cut/drive style when they are due to context."
    },
    {
      "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
      "text": "Shapers are defined by having positive residual structural influence; selecting extremes increases the risk of regression to the mean in any post-move metric, potentially exaggerating or masking differences."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "If multiple outcomes or cut/drive thresholds are tested, the false positive rate will increase unless corrected."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.35,
    "rejected": 0.05,
    "untestable": 0.40,
    "artifact": 0.10,
    "uncertain": 0.10
  },
  "decision": "do_not_proceed",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The difference in organizational role portability between shapers whose pre-move play-type mix is cut-dominant (cut share > drive share) and those who are drive-dominant, measured by the pre-to-post-move change in teammates' play-type mix displacement (Delta) or the stability of the shaper's own structural influence pattern, controlling for prior performance and team context.",
    "unit": "One shaper (low usage, positive residual structural influence) who moved between two NBA teams during the 2022-2026 seasons, with at least 15 pre-move and 15 post-move games and a minimum of 30 possessions each for cut and drive play types.",
    "sample": "All eligible shaper movers identified in the project's structural influence tables, using pre-move data from the season before the move and post-move data from the season after, with decoy players matched on position, usage, and prior team role to construct an expected null distribution. Expected n ≈ 24.",
    "power": "Simulation-based power analysis using the connector effect size (rho=0.27) indicates power < 20% for n=24; for 80% power, a true correlation of at least 0.55 would be required, which is larger than observed in any related study.",
    "controls": "Pre-move usage rate, age, position, prior team offensive rating, decoy-based expected displacement, and any injury indicators.",
    "falsification": "If cut-heavy shapers do not show significantly lower displacement than drive-heavy shapers (one-sided test), the null hypothesis of no difference cannot be rejected; also test whether any observed difference vanishes when using a placebo decoy sample.",
    "multiplicity": "Single pre-registered primary outcome (Delta or structural influence change) and a single cut/drive classification rule, with a pre-defined threshold (cut share > drive share) to avoid multiple comparisons. Any supplementary analyses will be labeled exploratory.",
    "decision_rule": "Proceed only if a pre-registered sample size of at least 80 shaper movers can be secured, for example by expanding the definition to include all low-usage, high-residual-influence players broadly (connectors and shapers combined) and by adding additional seasons. Without such an expansion, abandon the shaper-specific test as unpowered.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 through 2025-26 regular season and playoff play-by-play, with Neo4j play-type possession records as of 2026-08-08."
  }
}}

